Create the isolated practice network
On the host, inspect existing addresses, routes and virtual networks:
ip -brief address
ip route show table all
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-list --all
Check VPN and other active network prefixes too. If 192.168.231.0/24 overlaps an existing route, choose another unused private subnet and substitute it consistently below and in your notes. Do not remove an existing route to make room.
Save the following as blpractice.xml in a host working directory:
&lt;network&gt;
  &lt;name&gt;blpractice&lt;/name&gt;
  &lt;bridge name='virbr-blp' stp='on' delay='0'/&gt;
  &lt;dns enable='no'/&gt;
  &lt;ip address='192.168.231.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'&gt;
    &lt;dhcp&gt;
      &lt;range start='192.168.231.100' end='192.168.231.199'/&gt;
    &lt;/dhcp&gt;
  &lt;/ip&gt;
&lt;/network&gt;
There is no &lt;forward&gt; element. The bridge connects the host-side address and attached guests; DHCP supplies addresses in the stated range. DNS service is disabled for this first isolated network so it is not used as a host-provided external resolver. The libvirt network-format reference explains these elements and isolated-network behavior.
Confirm that both the network name and bridge name are unused. Then define and start this new network:
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-define blpractice.xml
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-start blpractice
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-autostart blpractice
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-dumpxml blpractice
Definition makes the network persistent; starting activates it now; autostart requests activation with the host’s libvirt network service. If definition or activation fails, read the error before continuing. Do not redefine another network or rename its bridge as an improvised repair.
In the guest, shut down cleanly with sudo poweroff. Wait until Virtual Machine Manager shows it shut off. In the guest’s NIC hardware settings, change the network source from the setup network to blpractice. Retain a single NIC. Apply the change, start bl01, and log in through the same graphical console.
Inspect the resulting guest view:
ip -brief address
ip route
The NIC should acquire a 192.168.231.100–192.168.231.199 address if you kept the example range. Record the actual address and interface name. Do not hard-code an interface name from someone else’s screenshot. External package downloads are no longer an expected operation in this phase. A displayed default route alone would not prove that external forwarding works; inspect the host network definition as well as the guest route.
On the host, confirm attachment with:
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system domiflist bl01
sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system net-dhcp-leases blpractice
The source must be blpractice. An absent lease might reflect timing or a guest DHCP problem; use the console to inspect the guest. Failure of one ping is not proof of isolation, because packet filtering can block a probe for unrelated reasons. Isolation evidence here is the one-NIC attachment, no forwarding configuration, no extra host bridge or routing setup, and the observed guest configuration.

CPU Pinning and NUMA-Aware VM Placement
To eliminate scheduling latency, context-switching overhead, and cross-socket bus contention:

Identify Host NUMA Topology:
numactl --hardware
# Node 0: CPUs 0-15, 64GB RAM
# Node 1: CPUs 16-31, 64GB RAM
Isolate Physical Cores for the Secondary Kernel: Host boot command line: isolcpus=domain,managed_irq,16-31 nohz_full=16-31 rcu_nocbs=16-31.
Pin QEMU vCPUs to Dedicated Physical Cores: Each guest vCPU thread is mapped 1:1 to an isolated physical core. In libvirt XML:
&lt;vcpu placement='static'&gt;16&lt;/vcpu&gt;
&lt;cputune&gt;
  &lt;vcpupin vcpu='0' cpuset='16'/&gt;
  &lt;vcpupin vcpu='1' cpuset='17'/&gt;
  &lt;vcpupin vcpu='2' cpuset='18'/&gt;
  &lt;!-- ... through core 31 ... --&gt;
  &lt;emulatorpin cpuset='0-1'/&gt;
&lt;/cputune&gt;
&lt;numatune&gt;
  &lt;memory mode='strict' nodeset='1'/&gt;
&lt;/numatune&gt;

Memory Ballooning vs Static Allocation
Default virtualization relies on memory ballooning (virtio-balloon) to dynamically reclaim memory from idle guests, and KSM (Kernel Samepage Merging) to deduplicate shared memory pages. Both must be disabled for multi-kernel bare-metal workloads:

Memory Ballooning Penalties: Causes random guest kernel page allocation latency spikes when the balloon driver inflates.
KSM Overhead: Periodically scans physical memory, invalidating page table entries and triggering TLB shootdowns.
Static Hugepages Allocation: Back the secondary kernel’s entire memory footprint with 1GB static hugepages allocated exclusively from NUMA Node 1:
echo 64 | sudo tee /sys/devices/system/node/node1/hugepages/hugepages-1048576kB/nr_hugepages

In QEMU/libvirt configuration:
&lt;memoryBacking&gt;
  &lt;hugepages&gt;
    &lt;page size='1' unit='G' nodeset='1'/&gt;
  &lt;/hugepages&gt;
  &lt;locked/&gt;
&lt;/memoryBacking&gt;
The &lt;locked/&gt; directive executes mlock(), guaranteeing the secondary kernel’s pages are permanently locked in physical RAM and cannot be swapped out under any circumstance.

Inter-Kernel Communication Patterns
For microsecond-scale inter-kernel coordination:

Inter-VM Shared Memory (IVSHMEM): Exposes a shared POSIX shared memory region (/dev/shm) directly as a PCI MMIO device inside secondary kernels. Instances read and write to the same physical RAM pages without any TCP/IP or network stack overhead (&lt;1µs latency).
Virtio-VSOCK: Zero-configuration socket family (AF_VSOCK) enabling point-to-point stream and datagram communication between host and guest kernels:
struct sockaddr_vm sa = {
    .svm_family = AF_VSOCK,
    .svm_cid = VMADDR_CID_HOST,
    .svm_port = 1234
};
connect(fd, (struct sockaddr*)&sa, sizeof(sa));
Bypasses IP routing, firewall tables, and packet serialization.

System Calls: Entry, Exit and Overhead
On x86_64, system calls enter via the SYSCALL instruction and return via SYSRET. The kernel saves caller-saved registers into a struct pt_regs structure on the task’s kernel stack:
// Simplified representation of pt_regs saved during syscall entry
struct pt_regs {
    unsigned long r15, r14, r13, r12, rbp, rbx;
    unsigned long r11, r10, r9, r8, ax, cx, dx, si, di;
    unsigned long orig_ax;
    unsigned long ip, cs, flags, sp, ss;
};

Parameter Passing: On x86_64, system call arguments pass in registers rdi, rsi, rdx, r10, r8, r9, and the syscall number passes in rax.
Dispatch Overhead: The kernel indexes into the sys_call_table array, executes the handler, and verifies return values.
Copy Overhead: For read/write operations, data must be copied between user buffers and kernel page cache buffers via copy_to_user() and copy_from_user(), validating memory bounds on each byte.

Memory-Mapped Files and mmap
Instead of copying data between kernel buffers and userspace buffers via read() and write(), applications can map files directly into their virtual address spaces using mmap():
// Map 1GB file into userspace address space
int fd = open("large_data.bin", O_RDWR);
void *addr = mmap(NULL, 1024*1024*1024, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);

mmap vs read() / write() Trade-offs

Zero-Copy Access: Memory-mapped reads avoid copying bytes through copy_to_user(). Userspace pointer dereferences directly touch the Page Cache frame.
TLB Shootdown and Page Fault Cost: Initial access triggers minor page faults to populate the process’s page table entries. On multi-threaded systems with frequent unmapping, TLB shootdown interrupts across cores can negate zero-copy savings.
Modern high-performance storage engines (e.g., LMDB, RocksDB, Kafka) select mmap or standard I/O based on working-set-to-RAM ratios.

bpftrace: High-Level Tracing
bpftrace is the premier high-level tracing language for Linux, inspired by AWK and DTrace:
# Measure block I/O latency distribution (visualized as a power-of-2 histogram)
sudo bpftrace -e '
  kprobe:blk_account_io_start { @start[arg0] = nsecs; }
  kprobe:blk_account_io_done /@start[arg0]/ {
    @latency_us = hist((nsecs - @start[arg0]) / 1000);
    delete(@start[arg0]);
  }'

# Trace slow filesystem sync calls taking longer than 10 milliseconds
sudo bpftrace -e '
  kprobe:ksys_fsync { @t[tid] = nsecs; }
  kretprobe:ksys_fsync /@t[tid]/ {
    $dur_ms = (nsecs - @t[tid]) / 1000000;
    if ($dur_ms &gt; 10) {
      printf("fsync PID %d (%s) took %d ms\n", pid, comm, $dur_ms);
    }
    delete(@t[tid]);
  }'

Writing Custom BPF Programs (CO-RE and libbpf)
Modern eBPF development uses Compile Once – Run Everywhere (CO-RE) with libbpf:

BTF (BPF Type Format): Embedded kernel debug metadata that allows eBPF programs to read kernel structs without needing kernel headers installed on the target machine.
Relocation: Clang and libbpf dynamically adjust struct field offsets at load time, ensuring a binary compiled on kernel 6.2 runs unmodified on kernel 6.8.

// Example CO-RE probe reading task_struct-&gt;pid safely
#include &lt;vmlinux.h&gt;
#include &lt;bpf/bpf_helpers.h&gt;
#include &lt;bpf/bpf_core_read.h&gt;

SEC("kprobe/sched_switch")
int BPF_KPROBE(trace_sched_switch, bool preempt, struct task_struct *prev, struct task_struct *next) {
    pid_t prev_pid = BPF_CORE_READ(prev, pid);
    bpf_printk("Switched from PID %d\n", prev_pid);
    return 0;
}
